#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158290} :activity/id #uuid "55c2127a-d43f-53c5-907e-c8d12316517c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048747 :topic/id #uuid "bac6604a-5262-5144-8ceb-19f17d508317" :topic/math-academy-id 2987} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158562 :tutorial/content "Suppose $X$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S.$\n\nThe values $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ are called the **first moment** and **second moment** of $X,$ respectively. \n\nRecall that the first moment $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is also called the *expected value* of $X$ and is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo find second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2],$ the expected value of the the random variable $X^2,$ we simply substitute $x^2$ for $x$ in the formula above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's compute the second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ of the continuous random variable $X$ with the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, \\qquad 0 \\leq x \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value of $X^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{7/2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2847fd82-e842-5ae8-889a-66f29c0442ec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6591 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3dc1ae12-0989-5596-8114-7d74427919d2" :step/math-academy-id 39753 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158292}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158291 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157393 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156994 :question/id #uuid "65957407-b302-51c5-93d9-0f71f3517362" :question/math-academy-id "e-10527"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c799d6-e3fe-5b78-986d-f2068b2db058" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050775}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508093}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Transformed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "897313ee-485e-51e5-8fec-b665d482b874" :step/math-academy-id 39754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157394 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156995 :question/id #uuid "292610ec-9c27-590d-b207-c76adc959d55" :question/math-academy-id "e-10528"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bada6a7f-c02e-51cd-8ea2-0594a94648a1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049801}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434425}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Moment of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "08325c1e-fe0c-5a7b-b6e5-8f27c2951461" :step/math-academy-id 39755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158294}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158293 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157395 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156996 :question/id #uuid "4c5666a4-f477-5b1a-8d17-1e2aa594863f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10529"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88bd32d5-4a84-58b3-a3e9-73bf32cb5e4f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479115}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Higher Moments of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "bce87273-1a01-58a6-825f-736ef62ef788" :step/math-academy-id 39756 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158291}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158563 :tutorial/content "It's also possible to define higher moments. For example, $\\textrm{E}[X^3]$ is the **third moment** of the random variable $X.$\n\nNotice the pattern:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *first* moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *second* moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^3] = \\int_S x^3 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *third* moment\n\nand so on. In general, the **$n$th moment** is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_S x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, note that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^2], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^3], \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nare sometimes referred to as the first, second, and third **raw moments**. This is because it's possible to define other types of moments.\n\nIn general, $\\textrm{E}[X^n]$ can be called either the $n$th *moment* or $n$th *raw moment*." :tutorial/id #uuid "e3527d5e-b0d3-5d76-aa5f-5bc7ffcfd66b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6592 :tutorial/title "Higher Moments"} :step/id #uuid "9fb3611b-a9ba-58f4-9159-00a7c9b29393" :step/math-academy-id 39757 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158293}}] :activity/title "Moments of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157533}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479151} :activity/id #uuid "f961c2fd-b375-5e2b-8cfd-ec028dc7aa0d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048747 :topic/id #uuid "bac6604a-5262-5144-8ceb-19f17d508317" :topic/math-academy-id 2987} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479149 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e8f87d-a86b-5e5a-9b4c-520af1d67778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478767} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5de172-0879-5846-8775-50301538400b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478775} {:answer/id #uuid "8c404e33-978f-5492-8afd-92388f08864c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478781} {:answer/id #uuid "92a6058f-3718-55b4-83a2-afc873b42494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478797} {:answer/id #uuid "a7862d49-f18d-5362-98d3-095ce5b8964a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478832} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b35dd5-9730-5404-b881-f84b63c0e854" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac78b028-e0aa-5c92-b54a-b1ba1527a82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-152915" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{4}(1-x),\\,-2\\le x\\le 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-[4+\\frac{32}{5}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{52}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "121da9a3-a27d-57e3-b7b6-52d9b4081634" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479157}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33cb4776-7d73-5e89-be66-3abb80c22cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427291} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5bf664-a18f-566d-a4f7-3bd738b55f2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427301} {:answer/id #uuid "74d74ac0-e401-549d-95e1-5b551db31568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427309} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2240d8-61b4-5dec-86e4-1ecedc83ab1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427322} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1156f5-fddd-5d6b-adf1-9732ee27312f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427333}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427291} :answer-field/id #uuid "62e8ebaf-8a75-5cb6-8621-5bcf5f7ac9e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "63bcffd8-dbf0-5c48-8ca6-56531c05d1c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-131141" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2x},\\,{e}^{-2}<x<1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}},$ compute $\\text{E}[4{X}^{2}-2X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[4{X}^{2}-2X]=4\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-2\\text{E}[X]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{e}^{-2}}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{e}^{-2}}^{1}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\,|}_{{e}^{-2}}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4}}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[4{X}^{2}-2X] & =4\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-2\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4}})-2(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}-1+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1ec8cc12-b2a4-5daf-96fd-1e378052ed2d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cfd005b-75a8-5b0f-ba56-4a0ed93ab33a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478604} {:answer/id #uuid "22afcee8-e19d-562c-b205-332bc6a88919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478613} {:answer/id #uuid "7a90c263-2858-513a-872f-0ae63784d35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478741} {:answer/id #uuid "edf857b5-69b2-5c68-8336-4b0f3c36ec60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478931} {:answer/id #uuid "ff85e4b7-ab02-503c-8793-d2b775c75049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478958}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478958} :answer-field/id #uuid "623ea09b-af47-50f2-9745-0713c7359869" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f6cddda8-c777-5283-b03a-3671533a782a" :question/math-academy-id "q-131153" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[10-4{X}^{2}].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\,1<x<2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[10-4{X}^{2}]=10-4\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=2$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[10-4{X}^{2}] & =10-4\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] \\\\  & =10-4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "46c499f6-0d50-5bd9-9f52-26ed8cb8ebb6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479153}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157606 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a69b927d-fd51-516f-aae0-cf0da09a7720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153881} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ba8dfc-c937-598b-a9a5-dc59f7c01e72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153882} {:answer/id #uuid "eb402453-8e9e-5d3c-a06e-96fbfc84d9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153883} {:answer/id #uuid "f418ec1e-ed9d-5bce-a47f-762f1c808098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153884} {:answer/id #uuid "63a0df93-12b0-5a94-8153-cab57c777d0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153883} :answer-field/id #uuid "07e9af8f-c53a-563f-b1d5-c1fb676708fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d40b16f4-c022-5679-aaee-aa1be46926d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-149708" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}+4}{5},\\,0<x<1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{20},$ compute $\\text{E}[2X-{X}^{3}+1].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X-{X}^{3}+1] & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{3{x}^{2}+4}{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{4})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{20}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]=\\frac{3}{10}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X-{X}^{3}+1] & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{11}{20}-\\frac{3}{10}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{10}-\\frac{3}{10}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "473a0974-6e51-5843-94d7-0cf17d7cd776" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479149}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1370ffbd-2eab-5260-a0b4-0bf5d3cdb6db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478582} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3f6cb5-53fa-5af5-81d3-830801107641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478750} {:answer/id #uuid "82d2a8f9-d415-5d71-a7d9-fe699cab072f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478758} {:answer/id #uuid "bedb09af-8e03-5cb2-a39f-8ac2f91ce3a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478867} {:answer/id #uuid "e89460a9-557b-5055-9a71-6a052e322419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478867} :answer-field/id #uuid "aded51f8-58f0-5888-884c-244971621142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2317ff10-80a3-5747-858a-863cca47fbc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-152922" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}, & 0\\le x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{x}){|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}(1-0)+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}+(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b0bf133b-cce4-5d5c-905a-ed7bcb9e9bdc"}] :activity/title "Moments of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478561}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508148} :activity/id #uuid "3d4cf227-5451-5675-a3d0-310e1e202b0c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048747 :topic/id #uuid "bac6604a-5262-5144-8ceb-19f17d508317" :topic/math-academy-id 2987} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8146a1-89ab-5805-b0d1-3cb41fad7d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{8}-1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434401} {:answer/id #uuid "77b5e97e-bc6c-5d70-b0ab-61c62f43eefe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434406} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c4a89b-c4d2-5db7-98e5-df1b9a18f200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434414} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd85839-7391-5b9a-80bf-f734af24df0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434415} {:answer/id #uuid "c0834f80-0b5a-5af1-a67a-b29a8e35f4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434416}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434401} :answer-field/id #uuid "29094946-ea57-508c-a265-54b76fd27fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a18a122-3ad0-51db-8855-5d861bdec3e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-152917" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2x},\\,1\\le x\\le {e}^{2}.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{4}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the fourth raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{4}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}({e}^{8}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{8}-1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0aef0e95-7b36-56cc-a917-a6909d024646"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508137 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e8f87d-a86b-5e5a-9b4c-520af1d67778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478767} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5de172-0879-5846-8775-50301538400b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478775} {:answer/id #uuid "8c404e33-978f-5492-8afd-92388f08864c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478781} {:answer/id #uuid "92a6058f-3718-55b4-83a2-afc873b42494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478797} {:answer/id #uuid "a7862d49-f18d-5362-98d3-095ce5b8964a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478832} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b35dd5-9730-5404-b881-f84b63c0e854" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac78b028-e0aa-5c92-b54a-b1ba1527a82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-152915" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{4}(1-x),\\,-2\\le x\\le 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-[4+\\frac{32}{5}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{52}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "62dbfd8d-d6c8-5022-be2b-1ea1fd60fadf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508118}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508141 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b2368f6-3f68-54b0-9d32-a27cbf3895be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507648} {:answer/id #uuid "b02c68e4-e61b-5654-9879-70de70a8fe6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-2e+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507843} {:answer/id #uuid "b269375e-588f-5e5f-9ae9-591c8a3221c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507845} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd68903-0ef9-5e2a-88ed-3677ac3ae07b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507864} {:answer/id #uuid "c01cb739-f7ab-5ebb-8e34-daf477537efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-5e+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507865}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507864} :answer-field/id #uuid "440eb13c-81c4-5c5d-9faa-611d1e590780" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b8f3b4b6-eb37-510a-a5f6-48850ac9d684" :question/math-academy-id "q-131140" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,1<x<e\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=e-1,$ compute $\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}-X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}-X]=2\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=e-1$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}-X] & =2\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}-(e-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-1-e+1 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "81573813-f419-5bab-b167-8255dfe1a85b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508148 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ed44a6-241d-5838-9fc9-af9b6f83e61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507622} {:answer/id #uuid "1578c936-8ad1-508e-aa56-b4990c519451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507624} {:answer/id #uuid "67abf3a2-af43-5891-a4b8-887a1c46a81b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507751} {:answer/id #uuid "7e517eaa-0474-52ae-938e-84dbfaa53de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507784} {:answer/id #uuid "8dda24db-7965-57a0-84c8-bf571ddd300c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507800}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507624} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5d3c470-c959-5590-9ddc-7ea57ee65776" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0cddfc6e-37ce-5b87-9aa0-8b3fda1d2938" :question/math-academy-id "q-90392" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}},\\,x>1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2},$ compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{3}{x})|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(3-\\frac{3}{a}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=3$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1 \\\\  & =3+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-1 \\\\  & =3+3-1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a28c654f-55ef-5916-869c-70e534152c19" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508141}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b58a9e1-f2e5-5bab-86c3-1dc62a5bf1dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434407} {:answer/id #uuid "83994b5b-dec3-525b-9473-71abebd50a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434408} {:answer/id #uuid "908f1924-a4cc-5cae-83ec-6a4c7872904f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434410} {:answer/id #uuid "93d5d0a4-79ad-5a4d-854e-315f284a4eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434411} {:answer/id #uuid "a25f9bac-857f-5e6a-8fee-76b7ccf2820e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434412}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434408} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c9c34c2-a686-5dff-8116-1e613117e2fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf5c8a43-29b3-589c-9d23-ef6cc2a81919" :question/math-academy-id "q-131142" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,1<x<3\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=2,$ compute $\\text{E}[5X-3{X}^{2}].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[5X-3{X}^{2}]=5\\text{E}[X]-3\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\,|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=2$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{13}{3}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[5X-3{X}^{2}] & =5\\text{E}[X]-3\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2-3\\cdot \\frac{13}{3} \\\\  & =10-13 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b234828c-5807-5de4-a2a5-5f231ccf4ede" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508137}}] :activity/title "Moments of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507600}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130036} :activity/id #uuid "b93dfde4-f3d0-56f7-90c3-7b5628613c8b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048800 :topic/id #uuid "c021e608-4c8b-5191-872f-f3e0b02d2d4a" :topic/math-academy-id 1983} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129173 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128992 :question/id #uuid "e17f982d-3d3e-5279-b507-d390e4f1970f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6116"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8537cc22-8d18-57c8-9ebf-921bd4d06ae7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048820}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366254}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Double Summation"} :step/id #uuid "ac75c745-5dbd-5824-9f7d-04a8faf6a86c" :step/math-academy-id 22705 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130377 :tutorial/content "Recall that we use **sigma notation** to write sums concisely. For example, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i\n$$\n\nrefers to the sum of all terms in the sequence $a_i,$ where the **index** $i$ ranges from $1$ to $n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i = a_1+a_2+\\cdots+a_n\n$$\n\nSigma notation can also represent sums whose terms depend on two indices. That is, **double sums** of the form \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^m \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $i$ ranges from $1$ to $m,$ and $j$ ranges from $1$ to $n\\mathbin.$ Thus, a double summation is just a \"sum of a sum.\" \n\nIn calculating double summations, we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we evaluate the inner sum by fixing the index of the outer sum ($i$) and incrementing only the inner index ($j$).\n\n- Next, we evaluate the outer sum by incrementing the outer sum index.\n\nTo illustrate how it works, suppose we are given the following double sum:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^2 \\sum_{j=1}^4 (i+j)\n$$\n\n \n\nTo compute this double sum, we can proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{4}(i+j) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}[({\\sum }_{j=1}^{4}(i+j))] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}[(i+1)+(i+2)+(i+3)+(i+4)] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}(4i+10) \\\\  & =4(1)+10+4(2)+10 \\\\  & =4+10+8+10 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLater, we will provide some formulas that will help us simplify many double summations calculations." :tutorial/id #uuid "3c842b98-cbdf-55a4-96a1-360697763430" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3867 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "583e493e-9206-508f-b782-7eec0883a7d5" :step/math-academy-id 22707 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130035}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130378 :tutorial/content "Double summations satisfy the following properties:\n\n**The Constant Multiple Rule** \n\nWe can factor out a constant multiple from a double summation:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n k a_{ij} = k\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}\n$$\n\n**The Sum Rule** \n\nWe can distribute a double summation over a sum of terms.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n (a_{ij} + b_{ij}) = \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} + \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n b_{ij}\n$$\n\n**The Double Sum of Units**\n\nRecall that for single summations, we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{j=1}^{n} 1 = \\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots+1}_{n\\textrm{ times}}  = n.\n$$\n\nAnalogously for double summations, we have that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n 1 = mn.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "41df0fcf-551a-58a5-b47e-ac1ddd121f7f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3868 :tutorial/title "The Sum and Constant Multiple Rules"} :step/id #uuid "3d0a00d2-9ccc-5261-bfa0-15e1bf5cb3c6" :step/math-academy-id 22708 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129174 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128993 :question/id #uuid "e8f3608f-68f2-5ba9-b618-447887e7f6fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6118"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4312c28-e298-5185-9009-e6ca243175fd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048922}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365122}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Sum and Constant Multiple Rules"} :step/id #uuid "42e12d98-e4bf-512d-b3c2-5c93584fc740" :step/math-academy-id 22710 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130196}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130195 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129259 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128835 :question/id #uuid "d180f745-028b-5859-8cad-4a49b5c20773" :question/math-academy-id "e-10521"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08375770-c610-5dae-b01b-c457de086cba" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371865}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule and Swap Rules"} :step/id #uuid "4e8c00d9-bc34-56d4-a71a-61e0e176e8fb" :step/math-academy-id 39727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130196 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130453 :tutorial/content "In addition, we have the following useful rules:\n\n**The Product Rule** \n\nWe can distribute a double summation over a product of *single-index* terms with *different* indices.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n  a_{i}b_j = \\sum_{i=1}^m a_i \\sum_{j=1}^n b_{j}\n$$\n\n**The Swap Rule** \n\nWe can swap the order of summations in a double summation.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}= \\sum_{j=1}^n\\sum_{i=1}^m a_{ij}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d25ceabe-e67a-59b3-9ed6-37be45644e5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6584 :tutorial/title "The Product and Swap Rules"} :step/id #uuid "5a28b905-cf2f-59e1-aaa2-6fb0064fcd4b" :step/math-academy-id 39728 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130195}}] :activity/title "Double Summations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129331}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134627} :activity/id #uuid "79df4a45-c922-5703-9054-3312861d012f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048849 :topic/id #uuid "c5e321c1-6c8d-5f19-b5bc-d257741addc3" :topic/math-academy-id 2095} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135031 :tutorial/content "The vector space $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ paired with the dot product, is called a **Euclidean space**. \n\nFor a vector ![](images/83/838ca0a3284985fa58d5ac894740b15f9bbbfd931299834cc8e062e364bbf983.svg) in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$  the **norm** (or **length**)  is given by \n\n$$\n\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert = \\sqrt{\\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}} = \\sqrt{x_1^2+x_2^2+\\ldots+x_n^2}.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector ![](images/2a/2a4e0070bdec6033ab8b71cbb77dbd8569bea8672c0e17cedbf876a77ce56bb2.svg) The norm of this vector is calculated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the dot product of a vector with itself is always non-negative, $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot\\mathbf{x}\\geq 0,$ so it's always possible to take the square root $\\sqrt{ \\mathbf{x}\\cdot\\mathbf{x} }.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cf55fe35-583a-57a7-9a58-529f60897c64" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3602 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4d2c70c6-8130-51bd-bba0-328eb720e63e" :step/math-academy-id 20183 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134628}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133759 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133568 :question/id #uuid "0ab996ae-e3a7-5353-87c2-9b2a01f4f808" :question/math-academy-id "e-5538"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d893f937-d802-5d27-b91a-b78caee0313e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049045}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423599}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector"} :step/id #uuid "41dc1ce1-3949-5bd4-a737-625779c43d60" :step/math-academy-id 20184 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134735}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133760 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133569 :question/id #uuid "f2ff1620-28e1-59ba-97ca-c43f3f300fdf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5539"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff66eef8-74b6-5553-af34-7da5a8cbbb02" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423597}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "b2bd1009-695c-5967-93d6-75e93251bdf5" :step/math-academy-id 20185 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134737}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135032 :tutorial/content "A **unit vector** is a vector whose length is equal to $1.$ If we divide a *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{x}$ by its norm $\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert,$ we obtain a unit vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{x}}{\\Vert\\mathbf{x}\\Vert},$ since\n\n$$\n\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert\t=\\left\\Vert \\dfrac{\\mathbf{x}}{\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert} \\right\\Vert\n\t\t\t\t\t\t= \\dfrac{1}{\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert}\\cdot\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert\n\t\t\t\t\t\t= 1.\n$$\n\nThe process of dividing a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ by its norm $\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert$ is called **normalizing the vector $\\mathbf{x}.$** We say that the unit vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{x}}{\\Vert\\mathbf{x}\\Vert}$ is *in the same direction* as $\\mathbf{x}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "65cfbc34-e842-5e88-aaa8-f40b697f1b1f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3603 :tutorial/title "Normalizing a Vector"} :step/id #uuid "33e38aa0-acfb-5a0e-87b4-4d4fdfb38455" :step/math-academy-id 20186 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134631}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133761 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133570 :question/id #uuid "7b7adf29-1653-5de7-915c-80a09f05995a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5540"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae41d849-9def-540c-8985-8fbeec679991" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423598}] :knowledge-point/title "Normalizing a Vector"} :step/id #uuid "cd0fcef8-cb65-59c0-81f7-f73c86d09b7e" :step/math-academy-id 20187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135073 :tutorial/content "In a Euclidean space, the **distance** between the vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the norm of the difference $\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{y},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\text{d}(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) = \\Vert \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{y} \\Vert.\n$$\n\nTo create a formula for the distance, we write the vectors in component form:\n![](images/01/011499b685af9a4c78d195755dc34e7b0d6ea0292eb2e9feafb94312fc7ee4b3.svg) \n\nNow, we can express the norm in terms of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}(x,y) & =‖x-y‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{y}_{2})^{2}+⋯+({x}_{n}-{y}_{n})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we can use the formula to find the distance between the vectors ![](images/2e/2e5d7cbac29024ca7f343363598714ea17bb180036d2f50f094a25584be2e23e.svg) and ![](images/37/37364e71c36f25701aa2e3b0ad368d4567b5719aa0c884ff0406ee9686125808.svg) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}(x,y) & =‖x-y‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{y}_{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-(-5))^{2}+(4-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "00353d62-b024-52a9-a1ad-744f7f9e1492" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3923 :tutorial/title "The Distance Between Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "c4535616-82fc-59ed-962a-9ca36d7214a8" :step/math-academy-id 23165 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134629}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133824 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133639 :question/id #uuid "86cf381d-8a69-5290-806b-09477c7ff92e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6190"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "837e0fa4-b8d3-59c1-87c7-44317adafaf9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423600}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Constant Out of the Norm"} :step/id #uuid "bba7150c-8928-5dd6-b8e9-d9d8983db01f" :step/math-academy-id 23169 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134630}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135074 :tutorial/content "We can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n\\Vert k \\mathbf{x} \\Vert= \\vert k \\vert \\cdot \\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector ![](images/43/430769431d512b87b6e67712fd8bd8de3802688f883600602a0d45b2749089cc.svg) To compute the norm of a multiple of $\\mathbf{x},$ say ${\\color{blue}-2}\\mathbf{x},$ we can factor the absolute value $|{\\color{blue}-2}|$ out of the norm as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-2x‖ & =|-2|\\cdot ‖x‖ \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We can verify that we'd get the same result if we multiplied the $\\color{blue}-2$ first, before computing the norm. Multiplying by ${\\color{blue}-2},$ we have\n\n![](images/5d/5da07173bda8f167d9a0609457fd10f1e3fdcdd2de498fd08c1c0035a205e5e1.svg)\n\nThen, computing the norm, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-2x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "128f0f2d-0adc-5542-b2a2-20d7a7ed6223" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3924 :tutorial/title "Factoring a Constant Out of the Norm"} :step/id #uuid "b0b507b6-b42b-5f48-ba2d-8f36454611f9" :step/math-academy-id 23172 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134736}}] :activity/title "The Norm of a Vector in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133982}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134644} :activity/id #uuid "b494fad7-04fa-5835-9125-27e96987941d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049951 :topic/id #uuid "20a638bb-c3f9-5b18-a306-728a49ef4638" :topic/math-academy-id 2099} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135037 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ be vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$. What do we mean when we say that the vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are orthogonal?\n\nTwo vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are **orthogonal** if their dot product equals zero: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=0\n$$\n\nIf two vectors are orthogonal, then we denote this by $\\mathbf{u} \\!\\perp\\! \\mathbf{v}$. For instance, the vectors \n\n![](images/29/29a81db4068e5bcc97fd38729ca19f11988a08e905bfc189e69c88357185ea31.svg)\n\nin $\\mathbb{R^2}$ are orthogonal, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is orthogonal to any vector $\\mathbf{u}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ since $\\mathbf{0}\\cdot\\mathbf{u}=0$." :tutorial/id #uuid "d602fe3a-c5cb-5b19-b348-5113b034e9f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3608 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "33af035b-34c4-553f-a53d-8f63666f0e02" :step/math-academy-id 20202 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134719}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133770 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133579 :question/id #uuid "7aec717f-6f52-590d-9cd0-47c34b587e1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5551"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ea335d6-6ee6-514f-acd4-deb39d50cf81" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423654}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components in Orthogonal Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "645df347-5e9c-54a1-993b-ebe8a9afb3af" :step/math-academy-id 20203 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133580 :question/id #uuid "ce2ff5a8-84bb-5d2b-8078-623203f1a3b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5552"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc757fa2-12ce-5a1c-a524-8eb256842079" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423655}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Dot Products Using Orthogonal Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "45beb215-0e09-5673-a4ed-c8979e0e7025" :step/math-academy-id 20204 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134647}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133581 :question/id #uuid "8b0122e7-3500-50cf-8505-22970428dac6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5553"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e02a242-6ef9-574a-be2a-2b68b35d761c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048982}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423656}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of a Vector Orthogonal to Two Given Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "a6e6ad37-9ff3-55fa-add6-c22d1ccbfd6c" :step/math-academy-id 20205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134719 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133630 :question/id #uuid "31abdef6-68e0-5c10-b03d-9d835ea6c681" :question/math-academy-id "e-5909"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce8958e6-c578-572f-8b34-5513b6f64a51" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049045}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423653}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Orthogonal Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "9cd9d03f-f5fd-5769-a0de-fec38d7515c2" :step/math-academy-id 21797 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134645}}] :activity/title "Orthogonal Vectors in Euclidean Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133986}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158130} :activity/id #uuid "d37ded1f-465b-5cac-8210-38b0f2560e8e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049989 :topic/id #uuid "2448a73d-2f61-51ae-8def-fd990d9c6f94" :topic/math-academy-id 2822} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157272 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157076 :question/id #uuid "2d12f13f-510a-558b-9e10-88151d3e1bb3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7077"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c108451-a023-5057-8e13-628be2df85cf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048778}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417327}] :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Statement by Distributing a Conjunction"} :step/id #uuid "c82bb8aa-643d-5e50-a35d-e9ff0334ef10" :step/math-academy-id 26061 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158107}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158100 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157077 :question/id #uuid "66490eb1-08f7-5df5-9540-88cc538e7d58" :question/math-academy-id "e-7078"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44bf5c8e-cf2a-595c-b93a-5fe338ffd766" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506584}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Statement Using the Distributive Property for Conjunctions"} :step/id #uuid "ee860d46-f0f0-59b9-9a98-146afd430ae9" :step/math-academy-id 26062 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158108}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157278 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157082 :question/id #uuid "726d5902-76f0-5321-bf3b-678fe487c831" :question/math-academy-id "e-7124"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5784bae8-d32b-522d-91c1-df798c1ad1d5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506587}] :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Statement by Distributing a Disjunction"} :step/id #uuid "6cd4b57c-f668-5ec7-be21-0bd6afcf4614" :step/math-academy-id 26194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158100}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158108 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157279 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157083 :question/id #uuid "ef6f99c1-d605-5b09-983d-05837b464e07" :question/math-academy-id "e-7125"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c12386-8f44-5a50-af97-25cb87ae649f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506592}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Statement Using the Distributive Property for Disjunctions"} :step/id #uuid "0df7beb5-c114-51ca-8751-453f137e4362" :step/math-academy-id 26195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158130 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158481 :tutorial/content "Just like we can distribute multiplication over addition, we can also distribute conjunctions over disjunctions (and vice versa).\n\n- We can distribute a conjunction over a disjunction, as follows:\n\n$$\nA \\land (B \\lor C)  \\equiv (A \\land B) \\lor (A \\land C)\n$$\n\n- Likewise, we can distribute a disjunction over a conjunction, as follows:\n\n$$\nA \\lor (B \\land C)  \\equiv (A \\lor B) \\land (A \\lor C)\n$$\n\nThe rules above are called the **distributive laws** for propositional logic. To prove that these laws hold, we can construct truth tables.\n\n- The following truth table shows that $A \\land (B \\lor C)  \\equiv (A \\land B) \\lor (A \\land C){:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,C\\,$ | $\\, A \\land (B \\lor C) \\,$ | $\\, (A \\land B) \\lor (A \\land C) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm 