" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387955} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6ce8cfd-2385-5069-885d-0df37da9dca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "38bb30f4-3f50-5deb-9dfd-dd66eda5da6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-128651" :question/problem "Which statement is equivalent to $(¬P∨Q)∧(¬P∨¬R)?$" :question/worked-solution "We can factor out the disjunction with $¬P,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n(¬P∨Q)∧(¬P∨¬R)≡¬P∨(Q∧¬R)\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "47a97766-cdb6-5556-90e5-d537c4ea4376"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b6a24a3-00d7-53b0-b984-428f6d5e7ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬H∨(K∧R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505959} {:answer/id #uuid "6e333ff7-a137-5519-b165-e838bb1a847d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(¬K∨H)∨(¬K∨R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506105} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8250eb-6363-5d94-8d29-e66175343aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(¬K∨H)∧(¬K∨R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506128} {:answer/id #uuid "96323502-f85c-5e82-8789-aa2fb7300f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬H∧(K∨R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506167} {:answer/id #uuid "ebef3221-e07a-583a-9066-5f6ee1be38b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(¬K∧H)∧(¬K∧R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506128} :answer-field/id #uuid "1897e0b0-e368-50c0-9420-52818418a17c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c7a7815a-c03d-5c73-81c2-1ce5ce9a6bba" :question/math-academy-id "q-86767" :question/problem "Which statement is equivalent to $¬K∨(H∧R)?$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute the disjunction over the conjunction, as follows:\n\n$$\n¬K∨(H∧R)≡(¬K∨H)∧(¬K∨R)\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "623ba644-95a4-5cc0-a0f5-ac76b4b3690d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506647}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a7826d2-20b0-547e-b606-5d696e04d920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(A∨¬B)∧C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506035} {:answer/id #uuid "96324f89-8360-53cb-a73f-a145a6b05dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(A∨¬B)∧(A∨C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506168} {:answer/id #uuid "a13d3e91-c57a-56f8-813d-a2ec08adca66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(A∨¬B)∨(A∨C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506202} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ae42f6-7c46-5f3b-b06e-5d399c2cb2d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(A∧¬B)∨(A∧C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506289} {:answer/id #uuid "d531e6fa-6661-5604-9db5-cddd549dadff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(A∨¬B)∧(¬B∨C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506292}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506168} :answer-field/id #uuid "95efb577-7be1-5cec-a87c-1e5bfca2015d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a1582560-8fbb-58c7-88eb-84a06eeb3625" :question/math-academy-id "q-128629" :question/problem "Which statement is equivalent to $A∨(¬B∧C)?$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute the disjunction over the conjunction, as follows:\n\n$$\nA∨(¬B∧C)≡(A∨¬B)∧(A∨C)\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b445dd0d-8061-5e06-9350-df2a654a8887" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506659}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9780ad-e843-5d43-a706-9c7d77bcb3ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C∧(¬B∧¬A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505986} {:answer/id #uuid "303103f3-ddbe-5c04-a865-1af3a7b85420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬A∧(¬B∨C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505997} {:answer/id #uuid "84dc2067-29f8-5336-a6c8-c340d50040a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬B∧(¬A∨C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506143} {:answer/id #uuid "9062a63d-7ecd-5732-82ee-6020ae3ee941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(¬B∨¬A)∧C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506156} {:answer/id #uuid "a21bfaeb-1b40-549b-a227-e65ae7ead397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬A∧(¬B∧C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505997} :answer-field/id #uuid "d72af412-04a9-5cd7-8234-1dd9272ec804" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b0451199-17d2-50c9-834a-20bc2c5dbac9" :question/math-academy-id "q-128643" :question/problem "Which statement is equivalent to $(¬A∧¬B)∨(¬A∧C)?$" :question/worked-solution "We can factor out the conjunction with $¬A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n(¬A∧¬B)∨(¬A∧C)≡¬A∧(¬B∨C)\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e7a9c0db-6080-5dbc-a0ed-bd3eae0539cb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506622}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5275ed32-e298-59cf-8ef0-17b68336e61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(Y∧Z)∨¬X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506051} {:answer/id #uuid "62913471-41fd-5e2b-9f88-320bc497733f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "X∧(¬Z∧Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506079} {:answer/id #uuid "6e82e878-5db5-51b9-a16f-b8ebd5161bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "X∨(Y∨¬Z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506109} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f32f07-2aca-53d0-b6de-7842d6594993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(¬Y∧Z)∨X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506264} {:answer/id #uuid "f0259b0d-5359-5467-926b-3197ad57682c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "X∨(¬Z∧Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506352}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506352} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0489a87-d66b-5ddb-becd-2d83ba7d6122" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dd8060ac-5133-5617-8826-8c5bef481505" :question/math-academy-id "q-81622" :question/problem "Which statement is equivalent to $(X∨Y)∧(X∨¬Z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can factor out the disjunction with $X,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n(X∨Y)∧(X∨¬Z)≡X∨(Y∧¬Z)\n$$\n\nNow, since $Y∧¬Z≡¬Z∧Y,$ we can write\n\n$$\nX∨(Y∧¬Z)≡X∨(¬Z∧Y).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fb1329da-957c-5193-8cb1-46919290659e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506628}}] :activity/title "The Distributive Laws" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505909}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120877} :activity/id #uuid "ea872253-9665-5737-9ff4-e1c4c5546bda" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050426 :topic/id #uuid "47f4b767-dd8b-5a07-a614-8ff85185d0eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1861} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121122 :tutorial/content "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = 0,$ then we say that the two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly dependent**.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider two sets of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/46/46c22d599752ded730a67bfd57b4dfe238e512f6dff2d561afaf2f145c8118c2.svg)\n\n- For the first set ${\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is true only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ is *linearly independent*.\n\n- On the other hand, for the second set ${\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has solutions other than $x_1=x_2=0.$ For example, $x_1=2$ and $x_2=1$ gives\n![](images/14/145f500e491087587478f03bd52ae180a7b4237acf17db0317ca07d28f389c56.svg)\nTherefore, the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ is *not* linearly independent. Rather, it is *linearly dependent*.\n\n**Note:** You may notice that the vectors in the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ were parallel (i.e. multiples of one another), while the vectors in set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ were not. This trend holds in general, for all sets of two vectors:\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel, while\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly independent* if and only if the two vectors are not parallel." :tutorial/id #uuid "938aba59-31f8-5ad9-8d3d-ce9287feb3de" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6811 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8b84b116-3328-5055-9d5b-02360cff09e6" :step/math-academy-id 41352 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121123 :tutorial/content "Any set of two vectors that contains the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. To see this, consider the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.$ We can realize that this set is linearly dependent using either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} + 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v} + x_2 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a solution $x_1=0$ and $x_2=1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set is linearly dependent.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, recall that a set of two vectors is linearly dependent if and only if the two vectors are parallel. That is, the two vectors are multiples of each other.\n\nThe zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to every other vector because it is a multiple of any other vector. For any vector $\\mathbf{v},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{0} = 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\mathbf{0} \\parallel \\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nSo, because $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{v},$ the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "ef2c96dd-d1cd-593e-a998-ba1be6c42686" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6812 :tutorial/title "Sets of Two Vectors Containing the Zero Vector"} :step/id #uuid "e814b4bf-688c-57df-8849-f4f2455723dd" :step/math-academy-id 41353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120879}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119497 :question/id #uuid "cbabcd64-6a33-552d-9f2f-704a9df44d1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10906"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048315}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423131}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"} :step/id #uuid "efae78f1-ab53-50ee-aa00-035536697ae6" :step/math-academy-id 41354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120880}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121124 :tutorial/content "So far, we've discussed linear dependence and linear independence for sets of two vectors. However, these concepts can be extended to sets of any number of vectors.\n\nA set of $n$ vectors $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is called **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + x_n \\mathbf{v}_n = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1=x_2=\\ldots=x_n=0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = \\ldots = x_n = 0,$ then we say that the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is **linearly dependent**.\n\nSimilar to sets containing two vectors, a general set of $n$ vectors is dependent if *any* two vectors in the set are parallel.\n\n**But be careful!** When working with sets containing more than two vectors, having a linearly dependent set is possible even if *none* of the vectors are parallel! \n\nFor example, consider the following set of vectors: \n\n![](images/bd/bd4df860628c44295dfd621e6b0e64839c2657c2baa094def656ea8b90ece9c8.svg)\n\nNone of the vectors in this set are parallel. However, we have\n\n![](images/50/5048e293fd6bb60a0ca95451aebd3bb77d0f748bb54e9f5dd044f60fb34c28ce.svg)\n\nSo, the equation\n\n![](images/8a/8ab4013ae0d9241268d040220efcd6184e23548e0685802cd6825b59a7bc71b1.svg)\n\nhas a solution $x_1 = 1,$ $x_2 = 1,$ and $x_3 = -1,$ which is different from $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that the vectors are linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "0d550a61-4219-5ffc-83a2-155ddd8fa9f4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6813 :tutorial/title "Linear Dependence and Independence for a General Set of Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "46556bbf-0804-5a49-af3c-3a10e60ee378" :step/math-academy-id 41357 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120881}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121125 :tutorial/content "As with sets containing only two vectors, any set of vectors that contains the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. \n\nTo see this, consider the set \n\n$$\n\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} + 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + x_{n-1} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} + x_n \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x_1=x_2=\\ldots = x_{n-1}=0$ and $x_n=1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=\\ldots = x_n=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "7270de59-1d83-5820-aa64-a5f4ca262e3e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6814 :tutorial/title "General Sets of Vectors Containing the Zero Vector"} :step/id #uuid "50295cf6-d08a-5be4-b71d-2116227de4f2" :step/math-academy-id 41358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120882}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119498 :question/id #uuid "7b28b42f-0ac4-543e-baeb-51534aaad480" :question/math-academy-id "e-10907"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381146}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"} :step/id #uuid "8247f0d4-142e-58a3-b2ed-d612accf2a6c" :step/math-academy-id 41359 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119499 :question/id #uuid "3843dc13-0908-5d4c-adfe-836a928a1410" :question/math-academy-id "e-10908"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433173}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"} :step/id #uuid "4922cdd4-be71-550d-b216-e5a30b232248" :step/math-academy-id 41362}] :activity/title "Linear Dependence and Independence" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120052}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134578} :activity/id #uuid "061434b6-5079-5b3b-b4da-2e73f02d7ea8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050750 :topic/id #uuid "6390206c-1add-5ea0-84f9-8288f56f20b5" :topic/math-academy-id 2163} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135186 :tutorial/content "The cumulative distribution function (or CDF) of a continuous random variable is defined the same way as for a discrete random variable. \n\nGiven a continuous random variable $X,$ the CDF is the function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x).\n$$\n\nIf our random variable has the probability density function (or PDF) $f(x),$ then we have\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(t) \\, \\textrm dt.\n$$\n\nNote that we use $f(t)dt$ in the integrand because $x$ serves as the upper limit of integration. \n\nFor example, consider the random variable $X$ with the following PDF:\n\n![](images/e7/e79c230f2b548c9280425f555f6d99a8f997e989289a05fb5f4584b656c4dcc8.svg)\n\nSince $f(x)= 0$ for all $x < 0,$  we have\n\n$$\nF(x)  = \\int_{-\\infty}^x 0 \\, \\textrm dt = 0\n$$\n\nif $x<0.$\n\nTo compute the CDF for $x\\geq 0,$  we integrate $f(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{2}{\\pi (1+{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\frac{2}{\\pi }(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing our CDF as a function defined over the entire real line, we get\n\n![](images/66/66b7bf54271075ae72fcf07fa4dc8573a94e97c2b2cc77680c0ec94b6053f360.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "de1bbdf6-687d-537a-a6fe-03d9fafbe841" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8364 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3f773be7-dfa4-524f-aec7-8ade124c5cea" :step/math-academy-id 132590 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134579}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133726 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133516 :question/id #uuid "38b4473a-f956-5af5-863b-387dc10b4924" :question/math-academy-id "e-13362"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d7509fa-a254-5b78-b307-3e0c4ef4d9ec" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424145}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a CDF Given a PDF With a Lower Bound"} :step/id #uuid "2411aa47-0b30-5e9c-b541-4d44b5f7aeed" :step/math-academy-id 132591 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133727 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133517 :question/id #uuid "98dd834c-ac74-5cdd-88ee-060fd9ac689d" :question/math-academy-id "e-13363"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b0ce833-e045-5aff-94a4-f9ad56cdb904" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501225}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a CDF Given a PDF That’s Nonzero Over All Real Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "f242a3a5-626f-54b6-b0cc-b8af4506a3f0" :step/math-academy-id 132594 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135187 :tutorial/content "When a random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive on the interval $a \\leq x \\leq b$ only, we have\n\n- $P(X \\leq x) = 0$ for $x < a,$ and\n\n- $P(X \\leq x) = 1$ for $x > b.$\n\nIn these cases, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n![](images/7a/7a64bac7c252a3f52fc92f6364edb5c66f31e47da28e78eeb0d069d894135700.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "0794419f-6b72-568b-a787-d71121c8bcd9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8365 :tutorial/title "Finding a CDF Given a PDF With Lower and Upper Bounds"} :step/id #uuid "a74ce991-870f-5bfa-8a96-8bbed06ce5b6" :step/math-academy-id 132597 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134582}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133728 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133518 :question/id #uuid "4159a407-ea9f-5728-b219-bf1afab732a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-13364"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eedcfa3d-b02b-5f96-99f4-cd0a452d272a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501183}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a CDF Given a PDF With Lower and Upper Bounds"} :step/id #uuid "47bc1334-f06b-5cc0-b191-427d1d862db4" :step/math-academy-id 132598 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135188 :tutorial/content "As with discrete random variables, we can use the CDF of a continuous random variable to quickly compute a probability over an interval:\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = F(b) - F(a)\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that a random variable $X$ has the following CDF:\n\n$$\nF(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}, \\qquad -\\infty < x < \\infty.\n$$\n\nWe can use the above CDF to quickly compute $P(0 \\leq x \\leq 1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le x\\le 1) & =F(1)-F(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-1}}-\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-1}}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ≈0.231\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval, so we have\n\n$$\nP(0 \\leq X \\leq 1) = P(0 < X \\leq 1) = P(0 \\leq X < 1) = P(0 < X < 1) = 0.231.\n$$\n\nIn general, for a continuous random variable $X,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = P(a < X \\leq b) = P(a \\leq X < b) = P(a < X < b) = F(b) - F(a).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e5203710-822b-55d4-9dfb-985bc4d41d6a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8366 :tutorial/title "Using a CDF to Compute a Probability Over an Interval"} :step/id #uuid "55418ca8-74f9-5755-9f75-2d411c4a476e" :step/math-academy-id 132601 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133729 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133519 :question/id #uuid "707e3b49-9c13-50d8-8a7f-c4d5ec44e0ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-13365"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbc399b-90f3-5acd-ac70-7345a8436978" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500293}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using a CDF"} :step/id #uuid "681dc276-df96-5b4d-b26c-338d9da62b88" :step/math-academy-id 132602}] :activity/title "Cumulative Distribution Functions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134025}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424891} :activity/id #uuid "dc977e81-fd96-5729-9752-b43ae05cc6d8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050750 :topic/id #uuid "6390206c-1add-5ea0-84f9-8288f56f20b5" :topic/math-academy-id 2163} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "492f777b-8264-55ab-9be1-d1359bd9513d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424871} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7a5540-6762-5260-9b7f-2a98061bf95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424872} {:answer/id #uuid "50e4b7f0-64ef-587b-bfde-a4520b836560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424873} {:answer/id #uuid "d60102cb-368c-5209-b3c3-06d9c44b89a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424880} {:answer/id #uuid "ef55c585-a616-50c9-9382-ffb7b5a8ceda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424881}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424881} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce55fccd-c2b9-544b-881d-b59a7e7c0f42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b70eaac-0c45-5da5-8a2f-2002d9071035" :question/math-academy-id "q-89727" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-|x/3|}}{6},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2},\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3f2429d9-8a43-540c-8ee1-21b8c07473a4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424890}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424890 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27f33f1e-6e04-5ef0-8a18-a9a9635e495a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424869} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc40166-55c4-53b3-bf06-562cddc8e868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424875} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0df617-e565-5181-bd27-09321bb7abfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424877} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e9b386-cf97-5734-82c8-f8b9318ea865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424878} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbc1dbf-3cbe-5835-99b1-f9050b0ed224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}-\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424882}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424875} :answer-field/id #uuid "5340e926-2580-5f2f-bf08-46d25e342346" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "97c91681-ff70-5452-bbfe-e1a92bb2e9d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126156" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{9}(x-1)(x-4),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $1\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}-\\frac{2}{9}(t-1)(t-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}-\\frac{2}{9}({t}^{2}-5t+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2}{27}{t}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{t}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}t)|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x-(-\\frac{2}{27}+\\frac{5}{9}-\\frac{8}{9}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9bf4404a-eca3-5f58-9aa8-59a46fdb29aa"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d43dadc-2415-595b-83bd-07293e62a4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424868} {:answer/id #uuid "2d728c61-ee7a-5242-b976-dff68318b24a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424870} {:answer/id #uuid "54302da3-4ffd-5ae6-9474-e5f95bb705f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424874} {:answer/id #uuid "86635ef4-b567-5842-a8be-7564b4bc47cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424876} {:answer/id #uuid "c53339f9-decf-5456-b59e-339275bad539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424879}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424870} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf2e7998-6dc3-5838-ae14-0d427c2b4449" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5d72a8e6-eda0-59be-829b-e0298d5412e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-89695" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{x}, & x\\ge 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(X\\le 5).$" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a continuous random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nHence, the required probability is $F(5)=1-\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{4}{5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b81f8974-1a7b-53cb-bb5f-1b47021e293e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424889}}] :activity/title "Cumulative Distribution Functions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424867}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442329} :activity/id #uuid "6e4f14fd-ece2-56c4-a9f7-319d868e1b1f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050750 :topic/id #uuid "6390206c-1add-5ea0-84f9-8288f56f20b5" :topic/math-academy-id 2163} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442326 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442322 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a555ce7-328a-5393-a56d-7007de7c9835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{63}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442306} {:answer/id #uuid "2cdbdc75-2141-5c85-914f-cbf2e3062225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{63}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442307} {:answer/id #uuid "4760a79e-f036-5d0a-936b-74020feb11e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442308} {:answer/id #uuid "921eed10-f9a7-592f-813f-d6d89b83f924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442312} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9281eb-aea1-5259-9259-3205cf4277fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{63}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442315} :answer-field/id #uuid "20b56563-d267-5cfe-8c9b-169cce28e14c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "365bf094-f659-5822-bf63-2e5a6992d995" :question/math-academy-id "q-89745" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}, & x\\ge 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(X\\le 2).$" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a continuous random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nHence, the required probability is $F(2)=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{6}}=\\frac{63}{64}.$"} :step/id #uuid "24fe79a9-1c18-5367-b5d5-50f26230d23d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442330}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442327 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c66a62f-9a33-540e-bb1b-485e50f03db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442303} {:answer/id #uuid "12f66496-4995-5d37-a001-943fa360d144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442305} {:answer/id #uuid "5de59784-191d-59f0-ab4e-cb2aafa6fe90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442309} {:answer/id #uuid "7295486c-b630-5ce0-8356-5ab47719adad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442311} {:answer/id #uuid "af97dd8a-6b84-534d-9a5b-b7c3f09bac24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442309} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0e871fb-e944-567c-b4fe-9835a0a9327f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e65ef0d7-6649-5f56-9e79-7407f7595f66" :question/math-academy-id "q-89621" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{64}x(16-{x}^{2}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability density function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $0<x<4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{64}t(16-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{64}(8{t}^{2}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4})|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}(8{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "443e51e5-e403-51ae-bbff-e563149f49e5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442326}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442328 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da4529e7-604e-506b-adaa-ff85312c20f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{-x^{2}+8x-7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442316}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442316} :answer-field/id #uuid "45ff772f-b783-5f66-89ac-69df3d1d8f2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "990f1bb6-e595-59df-8c02-d323344bc3ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-210785" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{9}x+\\frac{8}{9},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $1\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}(-\\frac{2}{9}t+\\frac{8}{9})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={(-\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}t)|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}x-\\frac{7}{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}({x}^{2}-8x+7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)(x-7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)(x-7), & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7a43148a-546c-5e25-817d-f6a542bfe3c4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a96b44b4-1f7e-576d-9e77-9ec278a83d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133273} {:answer/id #uuid "107b6d59-d74b-5f3c-9bc5-01a9d06b20b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133276} {:answer/id #uuid "105177ed-381e-5551-8d54-f794cc7ae2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442304} {:answer/id #uuid "c6834059-6614-531c-86f9-081bd2ed69b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442314} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ff0cb6-5ee6-5486-95b5-ba7fabd16b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442317}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442304} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3fece95-8a02-51e6-a7b8-03ddf8d52dc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d416c744-adfb-5c13-80ec-0e124bb7f438" :question/math-academy-id "q-89139" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ 1-{e}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(X>2).$" :question/worked-solution "We can perform this computation using the complement, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-F(2) \\\\  & =1-(1-{e}^{-2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "989b9a58-4363-5017-8054-1b3f417bd0d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442327}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442330 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65441b2f-7b3a-53c5-af33-45bf0ced0a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442310}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442310} :answer-field/id #uuid "60abe397-ada8-52f7-89c3-1ccbe2f29023" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7e1280de-34a4-5310-b0ea-3ca5ec3ba975" :question/math-academy-id "q-210763" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-|5x|},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2},\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{5t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-5t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cdf24c85-fec2-557e-990a-b96c38922ff0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442328}}] :activity/title "Cumulative Distribution Functions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442302}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500450} :activity/id #uuid "30d55716-f71c-501b-9f8c-41e45ad214a5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050750 :topic/id #uuid "6390206c-1add-5ea0-84f9-8288f56f20b5" :topic/math-academy-id 2163} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500401 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500304 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b2dedee-a9a6-5f08-aaca-929e3c0e1a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-8x-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499679} {:answer/id #uuid "215bd0d9-f220-5e68-82fa-30f7f2f20f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+8x-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499729} {:answer/id #uuid "6f65d4dc-2c09-5761-b57d-65243b8be70c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-8x-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499863} {:answer/id #uuid "9697c8ec-2113-54d6-875d-643ae67cf997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{-{x}^{2}+8x-15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499949} {:answer/id #uuid "ea81b801-f204-59da-86c8-5fb92620fdc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-8x-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499729} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4dd9342-bbc6-5cbd-86b8-630a593c1561" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "471d1d77-25cd-5101-acd9-2310f0d5d168" :question/math-academy-id "q-89672" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2(-x+4),\\, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<3 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $3\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<3 \\\\ {\\int }_{3}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{x}2(-t+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={8t-{t}^{2}|}_{3}^{x} \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+8x-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<3 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+8x-15, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4853d037-9366-5cfe-b2a6-ea749a958948" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500408}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500402 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e338a7c-f5c5-55b6-800b-47f10296b1d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{112}{729}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499686} {:answer/id #uuid "4673f4bc-028d-52bd-b6cb-43a313197f90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{1458}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499785} {:answer/id #uuid "60d5eb82-e934-5a50-abfb-6bb988e212e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{230}{729}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499844} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8b3642-18a5-59b9-ba90-c44b1dc2ff93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{130}{729}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499989} {:answer/id #uuid "b05849a6-fd14-5761-ac6a-f3df6f46c897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{113}{1458}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499844} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecc71117-bad7-5cf6-bbde-8b45ac8873de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2175ed2b-85f5-5f4e-9b21-fd44965b957e" :question/math-academy-id "q-126173" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1458}, & 0\\le x\\le 9 \\\\ 1, & x>9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(1<X\\le 3).$" :question/worked-solution "We can perform this computation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X\\le 3) & =P(X\\le 3)-P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & =F(3)-F(1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{6}-\\frac{{3}^{3}}{1458})-(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{1458}) \\\\  & =\\frac{230}{729}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4b98f3ca-9d65-5627-9573-4ba0b664bec7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500442}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500408 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "492f777b-8264-55ab-9be1-d1359bd9513d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424871} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7a5540-6762-5260-9b7f-2a98061bf95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424872} {:answer/id #uuid "50e4b7f0-64ef-587b-bfde-a4520b836560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424873} {:answer/id #uuid "d60102cb-368c-5209-b3c3-06d9c44b89a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424880} {:answer/id #uuid "ef55c585-a616-50c9-9382-ffb7b5a8ceda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424881}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424881} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce55fccd-c2b9-544b-881d-b59a7e7c0f42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b70eaac-0c45-5da5-8a2f-2002d9071035" :question/math-academy-id "q-89727" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-|x/3|}}{6},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2},\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "62b3bf85-d97a-532c-9a1f-d9d9bafa37cd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500402}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500442 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da4529e7-604e-506b-adaa-ff85312c20f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{-x^{2}+8x-7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442316}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442316} :answer-field/id #uuid "45ff772f-b783-5f66-89ac-69df3d1d8f2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "990f1bb6-e595-59df-8c02-d323344bc3ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-210785" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{9}x+\\frac{8}{9},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $1\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}(-\\frac{2}{9}t+\\frac{8}{9})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={(-\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}t)|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}x-\\frac{7}{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}({x}^{2}-8x+7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)(x-7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)(x-7), & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cc39bcea-e4ea-5193-946b-a894508db75a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500450 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095360e1-dd96-5383-931f-a201eff47a39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x)=1-\\frac{1}{1-{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419857} {:answer/id #uuid "170a0edf-da7f-5652-903d-c47b4ac8c342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x)=1+\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419864} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8d1627-2737-5252-95ce-1a7d32a52868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x)=1-\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419871} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0ae796-f204-5f28-ae72-68cb7f93938e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x)=1+\\frac{1}{(1+{e}^{x})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419873} {:answer/id #uuid "cfec970b-e672-5e0e-a27f-c56e83e7f7ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x)=1-\\frac{1}{(1+{e}^{x})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419918}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419918} :answer-field/id #uuid "f84d8dbb-e4a3-55af-a310-662430392d90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ae38bc39-2bd5-58c3-af2b-ad65de482c6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-89635" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{(1+{e}^{x})^{3}},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a continuous random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{(1+{e}^{t})^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{(1+{e}^{t})^{2}}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1+{e}^{x})^{2}}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{(1+{e}^{x})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e7bca5f9-6ddb-5ca7-b984-fdd1e81274c6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500401}}] :activity/title "Cumulative Distribution Functions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499644}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501258} :activity/id #uuid "080db2d3-dbc7-5528-9498-6b1144f7a367" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050750 :topic/id #uuid "6390206c-1add-5ea0-84f9-8288f56f20b5" :topic/math-academy-id 2163} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e338a7c-f5c5-55b6-800b-47f10296b1d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{112}{729}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499686} {:answer/id #uuid "4673f4bc-028d-52bd-b6cb-43a313197f90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{1458}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499785} {:answer/id #uuid "60d5eb82-e934-5a50-abfb-6bb988e212e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{230}{729}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499844} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8b3642-18a5-59b9-ba90-c44b1dc2ff93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{130}{729}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499989} {:answer/id #uuid "b05849a6-fd14-5761-ac6a-f3df6f46c897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{113}{1458}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499844} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecc71117-bad7-5cf6-bbde-8b45ac8873de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2175ed2b-85f5-5f4e-9b21-fd44965b957e" :question/math-academy-id "q-126173" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1458}, & 0\\le x\\le 9 \\\\ 1, & x>9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(1<X\\le 3).$" :question/worked-solution "We can perform this computation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X\\le 3) & =P(X\\le 3)-P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & =F(3)-F(1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{6}-\\frac{{3}^{3}}{1458})-(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{1458}) \\\\  & =\\frac{230}{729}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1943e8ec-2299-593e-84f2-5af6e5f545e6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501283}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501258 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c522a9a-a340-5730-935a-e853421c44ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{75-2x}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500525} {:answer/id #uuid "49f3c406-0b5e-5522-a240-414ac7e0f856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500615} {:answer/id #uuid "887364d0-5dcf-5d1a-9cb5-af1322ef590e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{75-{x}^{2}}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500754} {:answer/id #uuid "b55dc857-62d1-5ed0-85ee-8b7c291b0260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{75}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500845} {:answer/id #uuid "f364853a-396b-5d86-bdbe-5ffe621626d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{75}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500984}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500615} :answer-field/id #uuid "94adf626-a400-5181-b287-7ccffcedb8aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "770dc96c-77d3-556b-b303-18d387043365" :question/math-academy-id "q-89606" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{75}x,\\, & 5\\le x\\le 10 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<5 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 5\\le x\\le 10 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $5\\le x\\le 10,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<5 \\\\ {\\int }_{5}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 5\\le x\\le 10 \\\\ 1, & x>10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{5}^{x}\\frac{2}{75}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{1}{75}{t}^{2}|}_{5}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{75}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<5, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{75},\\, & 5\\le x\\le 10, \\\\ 1,\\, & x>10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "242c6578-3fc8-593e-975a-c9f65e32ad15" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501263}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501259 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e28268-65ac-57f8-af23-8c479907164b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3})}{\\pi }+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500575} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2d1618-08b3-504b-b2c5-01edf31c29f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3})}{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500592} {:answer/id #uuid "57509640-0482-58cb-88f2-e1a8054d767c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3})}{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500644} {:answer/id #uuid "697f3908-8152-5679-962d-8baf5ef9a307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3})}{\\pi }+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500685} {:answer/id #uuid "acccdbc8-03e2-5723-966d-27a022e016ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3})}{\\pi }-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500823}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500592} :answer-field/id #uuid "b07d72d2-93c7-594a-8963-dcc12be62f0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b31007d2-c9e1-51d3-8e66-44402735c224" :question/math-academy-id "q-89634" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\pi (1+{x}^{6})},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a continuous random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{\\pi (1+{t}^{6})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({t}^{3})}{\\pi }|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3})}{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "30e12182-d109-5cee-b62b-e22ed003514c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501263 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f4af3cf-f231-595b-aaf4-49e10d20ff41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133280} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bd978c-da5d-59dd-85e1-a2c03295ee58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133281} {:answer/id #uuid "f256f7ab-c378-584b-ab95-d8f9f46921ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133282} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3624d2-d1b2-5d89-8864-4bd4b45113e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{8}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500616} {:answer/id #uuid "c3226fc1-cbce-5f81-a7bd-59cad1dcc127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\frac{8}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500875}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133281} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2ed936f-3ea8-56c4-8eab-fb9925367eff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dc0b739c-e444-5ab3-bcd2-409b731d56fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-89612" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}, & 0\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1, & x>5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(1\\le X\\le 3).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval. So we can perform the computation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 3) & =P(1<X\\le 3) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 3)-P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & =F(3)-F(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "39d0ba9a-b08d-5b45-b5bf-8a3943de2319" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501259}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501283 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b593b2a-af8d-5c4e-9746-8d5e8f4120fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{e^{-x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500523}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500523} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb588555-9fd1-5660-8555-bc1960f97262" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d0e0e171-2942-5666-9f4c-984a9e7d868a" :question/math-academy-id "q-185531" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-|x|}}{2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2},\\, & x\\le 0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2},\\, & x\\le 0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2},\\, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b33ab878-bbe3-5d53-9e65-806860542c47"}] :activity/title "Cumulative Distribution Functions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500457}]}
